WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.560
The penultimate episode of "Loki" on Disney+&nbsp;
was a wild, Variant-filled ride that set up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.520
what is sure to be a mind-blowing finale.&nbsp;
It was also jam-packed with fun Easter eggs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.560
for fans to find. Here are a few you may have&nbsp;
missed in Episode 5, "Journey Into Mystery."

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.960
At the beginning of Episode 5, we travel through&nbsp;
a destroyed cityscape full of decrepit buildings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.280
Most of these structures seem like filler to&nbsp;
emphasize the destruction, but there's one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.400
significant Easter egg hidden within the rubble&nbsp;
that's worth noting. Toward the end of the camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.640
pan, a building on the right reads "Qeng" —&nbsp;
"Q-E-N-G" — at the top of it. Marvel Comics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.160
fans should know this famous name as it's related&nbsp;
to a major villain who's set to appear in the MCU.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.040
This tower represents Qeng Enterprises, and if&nbsp;
you thought it looked like the Avengers Tower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.840
you weren't wrong. In the comics, it's revealed&nbsp;
that Stark Industries sold the building to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:01.040
company in "Avengers" Vol. 6 #0 from 2015.&nbsp;
The CEO of Qeng Industries is Mr. Gryphon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.560
aka Nathaniel Richards, who exists on&nbsp;
Earth-6311 — an alternate version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:10.640
Earth-616, the reality where all&nbsp;
the main Marvel stuff goes down.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.120
Nathaniel Richards is also known as Kang&nbsp;
the Conqueror, as the latter was split into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.080
two different versions of himself due to a&nbsp;
timestream error. It's a complicated story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.600
from 2015's "All-New, All-Different Avengers"&nbsp;
#1, but this nod to Kang — a major villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.720
we know will appear in "Ant-Man and the Wasp:&nbsp;
Quantumania" — is something that we can't ignore.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.400
During Episode 5, one of the first things we&nbsp;
see in the Void is a menacing purple-black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.560
cloud with a monstrous face within it.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.240
"This is The Void. That's Alioth.&nbsp;
And we're his lunch. Come on!'

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.400
Known as a trans-temporal entity,&nbsp;
Alioth doesn't exist in any reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.720
but instead what the comics describe&nbsp;
as Temporal Limbo. The being is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:57.200
biggest enemy of Kang the Conqueror,&nbsp;
and its empire is bigger than his foe's.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.960
Alioth first appeared in 1993's "Avengers: The&nbsp;
Terminatrix Objective" #1 and is known for its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.480
absorption powers, which we saw play a huge&nbsp;
role in Episode 5. Alioth is drawn to living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.600
beings more than anything and has the power [to&nbsp;
disintegrate them as soon as it encompasses them.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.320
In the MCU, it appears as if Alioth&nbsp;
lives within the Void at the end of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.040
unlike its location in the comics. It's also&nbsp;
being used to protect the person or entity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.400
behind the Time Keepers, who many now suspect&nbsp;
to be Kang. If this is the case in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.720
the two would be on the same side, a&nbsp;
massive shift from their comic book origins.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.040
In a move we never thought would happen, the&nbsp;
Thanos-Copter became canon in the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.880
Universe thanks to "Loki" Episode 5. When the Mad&nbsp;
Titan finally perished in "Avengers: Endgame,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.680
it looked like we might never see the famous&nbsp;
aircraft, but now, we know why we never did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.800
The Thanos-Copter first appeared in&nbsp;
1979's "Spidey Super Stories" #39&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.720
and was a helicopter the villain&nbsp;
used one time to attack Hellcat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.360
a popular Marvel hero who fought the Titan&nbsp;
as he tried to obtain the Cosmic Cube.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.760
Thanos also picked up Deadpool in the helicopter&nbsp;
in Deadpool Vol. 5 #45, when the latter was hired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.080
to help the Mad Titan steal the cube. It's&nbsp;
such a shame we never got to see that scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:19.440
play out on the big screen. Fortunately, "Loki"&nbsp;
Episode 5 paid homage to a great — and frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.320
mocked — comic book moment. The Thanos-Copter&nbsp;
matched its comic book appearance exactly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.640
with its bright yellow paint and the Titan's&nbsp;
name written across it in large bold letters.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.840
We knew we'd be getting a version of the trickster&nbsp;
god from the classic "Vote Loki" comic after he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.120
appeared in several trailers for the series. And&nbsp;
he finally arrived in Episode 5 but only for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.320
brief scene where a scuffle breaks out between&nbsp;
a gaggle of Lokis. As for the variant's origin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:49.920
story, the four-part comic series debuted in&nbsp;
June 2016 and was a satire on the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.440
presidential election of the same year. In the&nbsp;
fourth and final issue, Loki concedes the race.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.000
In the comics, Loki's campaign is based on&nbsp;
his honesty about how he's going to lie to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.200
American public. The citizens eat it up, except&nbsp;
for a reporter from the Daily Bugle, who's trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.000
her best to take him down. It's definitely&nbsp;
one of the weirdest Loki stories of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.080
complete with "Political Action Cults" sacrificing&nbsp;
goats. And we're incredibly glad this dapper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:20.720
politically ambitious Loki got to show up on the&nbsp;
screen, even if it was only for a few minutes.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.480
Just before the Loki group makes their way down to&nbsp;
their secret hideout, we see different layers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:32.000
rubble above their little base. And buried in&nbsp;
all this debris, we get a glimpse of Mjolnir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.760
and a small glass jar with "T365" written on&nbsp;
it. Trapped inside this jar is a tiny version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.560
of Thor ... and one who's a bit more amphibious&nbsp;
than we're used to seeing the God of Thunder.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.560
Known as Throg, this powerful frog version&nbsp;
of Thor first appeared in "Thor" #363,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.560
although #365 is more significant&nbsp;
for the character because it's where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.080
he lifted Mjolnir and became&nbsp;
the Thunder Frog officially.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.000
This coincides with Mjolnir being just&nbsp;
out of Throg's reach in the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.120
as he's trapped in the glass jar.&nbsp;
"Thor" #365 also heavily features Loki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.760
who suspects something is afoot with his brother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:13.280
It's a hilarious storyline which includes Thor&nbsp;
helping an army of frogs battle an army of rats.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.040
Just as with D.B. Cooper in Episode 1,&nbsp;
Episode 5 of "Loki" further explained some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.400
great American mysteries. The first to appear&nbsp;
in "Journey Into Mystery" was "Polybius" — an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.960
arcade game long considered an urban&nbsp;
legend. The legend of "Polybius" suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:34.320
this fictitious '80s game was real and run by&nbsp;
the government as a psychological experiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.000
It was supposedly created to test mind control&nbsp;
on users, but the arcade game was never found.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.040
It looks like it was swooped up by the TVA and&nbsp;
sent to the place where urban legends go to die.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:50.240
In the middle of "Episode 5," we also see&nbsp;
the USS Eldridge fall out of the sky and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:56.240
into the Void. Unlike "Polybius," the famous&nbsp;
naval ship was an actual thing. In 1943,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:02.160
it was alleged that the ship was made invisible&nbsp;
as part of the famous Philadelphia Experiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.720
As some claim, it was also apparently&nbsp;
teleported from Philadelphia to Virginia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.680
and back again. Crew members who were on&nbsp;
board reportedly went insane, and the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:17.360
was so bizarre it was adapted into a time-travel&nbsp;
movie — "The Philadelphia Experiment" — in 1984.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:21.520
Since Episode 5 mostly took place&nbsp;
in the Void, set designers had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.080
major opportunity to include an incredible&nbsp;
amount of Easter eggs from the MCU's past.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.920
Some of these include "Ant-Man" villain&nbsp;
Yellowjacket's helmet in the first act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:34.880
which we saw as we panned over the landscape,&nbsp;
and Ronin's Dark Aster spaceship also makes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:40.240
brief appearance. We also get a brief look at a&nbsp;
demolished Helicarrier sitting in the wasteland.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.480
Also, in the vast devastation across&nbsp;
"Loki" Episode 5 is what appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:50.400
be a stone face of the Living Tribunal —&nbsp;
which we know isn't an MCU relic ... yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.400
One of the most important entities in Marvel&nbsp;
Comics is the character who first appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:01.360
in "Strange Tales" #157 from 1967. The Living&nbsp;
Tribunal basically oversees everything, ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.320
in every reality. Serving as somewhat of a judge&nbsp;
of the entire cosmic universe and all of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.440
realities, the Tribunal is more powerful&nbsp;
than all of the Infinity Stones combined.

